                  <provenance type="#e"><orgName role="fmo" key="org_307">Lessness or Westwood,
                     Kent. Augustinian abbey of St Thomas the Martyr</orgName><p>'Hic liber
                     [...Tho]me martiris [de Liesnes] quem qui ei abstulerit uel fraudem ei fecerit
                     nisi eidem ecclesie plene satisfacerit (sic) anathema sit maranatha Amen'
                     (erased at bottom of fol. 1r).</p></provenance>
                  <provenance type="#rejected"><orgName role="fmo" key="org_474">St Albans,
                     Hertfordshire, Benedictine abbey of St Alban</orgName> <p>This attribution was
                     rejected in the second edition of Medieval Libraries of Great Britain (1964),
                     which did not change in the Supplement (1987). The corresponding Medieval
                     Libraries of Great Britain index card indicates that M. R. James was the source
                     of this attribution. In James's words: <q>Possibly St Alban's (or possibly
                     Belvoir) is the source of the volume; the inscription is conceived on just the
                     same lines as in St Alban's books</q>. Neil Ker added a note to the index card
                     refuting James's theory and redirecting to Lessness: <q>But maranatha is <hi
                     rend="italic">not</hi> found in St Alban's books; from
                     Lessness.</q></p></provenance>
               </history>
